# llm-call-audit

Finds every place an app calls an LLM and every place it pays for the same tokens twice: prompts the provider's cache could have reused and did not, and output the app generated and then threw away. Each finding is tied to a rule, a file and line, an estimate of the tokens it loses per 1,000 calls, one fix, and a way to verify the fix. The result is a report, `leanprompt-report.md`, a findings file, `.leanprompt/findings.json`, and a score from the leanprompt CLI.

The audit owns no rules of its own. It finds the calls and applies the rules of the other four skills:

- [prefill-cache](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/prefill-cache/SKILL.md): `LP-PC-*`, prompts the cache can reuse.
- [generation-economy](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/generation-economy/SKILL.md): `LP-GE-*`, calls and output that are not wasted.
- [lmwarden](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/lmwarden/SKILL.md): `LP-LW-*`, self-hosted servers.
- [measure-loop](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/measure-loop/SKILL.md): how each fix is proved, and the score.

The audit never edits code without asking. It proposes one fix per finding, the user decides, and each applied fix is handed to `measure-loop` to prove.

Ask first: before you edit the app's code, install a package, add a file outside `.leanprompt/`, or run anything that sends requests to a provider (they are billed), show the change or the command and ask. The user may prefer to run it themselves. No ask is needed for the audit's own `leanprompt-report.md` and `.leanprompt/findings.json`, or for `npx leanprompt score` and `npx leanprompt calibrate`, which only read and write files under `.leanprompt/` and make no network calls (`calibrate` still writes `.leanprompt/weights.json` only after `y` or `--yes`). Offering to share a lesson (below) needs no ask, but running `npx leanprompt lesson` does: it writes `.leanprompt/lesson.md`, so run it only after the user says yes.

## When to use

- The user asks to audit an LLM-calling app, to cut its token bill or latency, or to find out why its provider reports few cached tokens.
- The user wants a leanprompt score or the README badge.
- A new codebase arrives and you want to know what it calls, with which model, and how.

For a single change you are writing right now (a new prompt builder, a new retry), use `prefill-cache` or `generation-economy` directly; the audit is for the whole app.

## Workflow

1. **Know the target and priority.** Read `leanprompt.yaml` at the project root ([docs/profile.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/docs/profile.md)): `target` (provider or self-hosted engine, model family, context length), `engine` (self-hosted cache facts), `priority` (cost, speed, quality or weights) and `ignore`. If the file is absent, infer the target from the code ([call-shapes.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/llm-call-audit/call-shapes.md), "Which provider and model") and ask for what the code cannot tell you, above all the priority; then offer to save the answers to `leanprompt.yaml` so they are not asked again, show the lines that would be written, and write them only on a yes ([presenting.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/llm-call-audit/presenting.md)). For an LM Warden server, offer `npx leanprompt profile detect --lmwarden <url>`, which reads the engine facts from the server instead of asking (the [lmwarden](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/lmwarden/SKILL.md) skill describes it).
2. **Inventory the calls.** Find every call site with [call-shapes.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/llm-call-audit/call-shapes.md): SDKs, frameworks, raw HTTP, and the app's own wrappers. For each, record file and line, provider, model and call kind (chat, agent loop, one-shot, batch), and follow it back to where its system prompt, tools, history and request options are built.
3. **Check the rules.** For each call site, walk the parts of the request through the rule map below and open a rule file when its "Detect in code" pattern matches. Raise a rule only when its `applies_to` frontmatter includes the target, and drop any finding whose rule and file are on the `ignore:` list.
4. **Read the usage, if there is any.** If `.leanprompt/usage.jsonl` exists, run `npx leanprompt score` first and read its inputs and its heads diagnostic (below); they confirm, correct or add to what the code shows.
5. **Estimate and rank.** Give each finding an estimate of the tokens it loses per 1,000 calls (below), labelled as an estimate or as measured, and rank by it. Before proposing any cache mark, apply the break-even rule (below).
6. **Write the files.** Write `.leanprompt/findings.json` (format below), run `npx leanprompt score`, and write `leanprompt-report.md` at the project root from [report-template.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/llm-call-audit/report-template.md).
7. **Present and decide.** Show the summary first, then one finding at a time as a decision, as [presenting.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/llm-call-audit/presenting.md) describes. For each finding the user wants fixed, show one fix as a small diff and apply it only after the user says yes.
8. **Prove each fix.** Ask before running anything that sends requests or adds files, then hand over to `measure-loop` (below), mark the finding `fixed` and rescore.
9. **Offer calibration** after a static score (below).
10. **Offer to share a lesson** after a measured result or a rule that misled (below).

