An `lm-warden` target counts as `vllm` when you match `applies_to`, as it does for the cache tables. With no known target, check every rule and say which ones depend on the provider.

The priority changes the order and the advice, not what counts as waste: follow the priority-aware advice in [generation-economy](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/generation-economy/SKILL.md). Caching never changes answers, so the `LP-PC-*` findings apply under every priority. Findings that change price or latency but not tokens (a batch API, LP-GE-07; a smaller model, LP-GE-08) have no tokens lost to rank by; list them after the token findings, in the order the priority gives them.

## Estimating tokens lost per 1,000 calls

Findings are ranked by the tokens each one loses per 1,000 calls of the whole app, so a large leak on a rare call ranks below a small leak on every call. Write each number with its kind: "(estimate)" when it comes from the code, "(measured, N calls)" when it comes from the usage log.

With no usage log, estimate it from the code, as the product of three things, and say it is an estimate:

1. **Tokens lost on an affected call.** For a prefill finding, the size of the part of the prompt the cache could have reused and now recomputes: everything from the first byte that changes to the end of the otherwise stable part, in the provider's render order (the "Why" of each rule gives the order). For a generation finding, the output tokens of the call that is repeated, retried or thrown away, or the prompt tokens of a context sent once per question or sample (LP-GE-01, LP-GE-02). Count tokens with the provider's tokenizer or token-counting endpoint when you can; otherwise estimate from the text's length and say so.
2. **How often a call is affected.** A value that changes on every call (a timestamp, a request id) affects every call; a value that changes per session or per user affects the first call of each session or user; a history rewrite affects every turn after it; a retry on truncation affects the calls that hit the limit.
3. **This call site's share of the app's calls,** from the call kind: an agent loop makes many calls per task, a title or label call one per conversation. When you cannot tell, treat the call sites as equal and say so.

Apply the provider's limits before you multiply: a prefix shorter than the provider's minimum cacheable size loses nothing, and a self-hosted engine reuses only whole blocks. Those numbers are in [providers/](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/tree/v0.1.0-dev/providers/) and in `leanprompt.yaml`'s `engine:` block; read them there.

With a usage log, measure instead. `.leanprompt/usage.jsonl` holds `prompt_tokens`, `cached_tokens` and `output_tokens` per call and no file or line, so match records to a call site by provider, model and `ids.head`, and count the prompt tokens that were not cached on calls that continue a conversation, and the output tokens of repeated, retried and parse-failed calls ([generation-economy](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/generation-economy/SKILL.md), "Reading the usage record"). Where a record cannot be matched to one call site, keep the code estimate for that finding.

## The break-even rule for cache marks

Before recommending a cache mark, apply the break-even rule in [LP-PC-01](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/prefill-cache/rules/LP-PC-01.md): where the provider charges a premium for cache writes, a prefix that is not read again within the cache lifetime costs more marked than unmarked. Estimate how often each marked prefix returns within the lifetime: from the usage log, by the time between records with the same `ids.head` or `ids.nodes` prefix; from the code, by the call kind (a chat or agent loop returns within seconds, a one-shot prompt may never return, a nightly job returns once a day). Then advise one of four things: mark, don't mark, longer lifetime, restructure (move the varying part after the stable one so a mark can cover more). The write and read prices and the lifetimes are in [providers/anthropic.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/providers/anthropic.md) and the other provider tables; LP-PC-11 covers pauses longer than the lifetime.

## The heads diagnostic

After `npx leanprompt score` writes a measured score, read `diagnostics.heads` in `.leanprompt/score.json`: `distinct_head` is how many distinct heads (system prompt and tools together) the log holds, and `conversations` how many distinct conversations. An app whose call sites each send one fixed head should show about as many distinct heads as it has distinct prompts (roles, agents, call sites), however many conversations it logged. Many distinct heads for the conversation count, growing as `conversations` grows, means something per session, per user or per compaction is in the head. The score cannot see this: its stability figure counts only requests that continue a conversation, so a head that differs between conversations costs reuse on every first request without lowering it (E4, E5). Look for LP-PC-03 (a per-session value at the top of the prompt), LP-PC-07 (per-user context before shared context) and LP-PC-08 (compaction that rewrites the system prompt and starts a new conversation), and raise the one the code shows, with the counts as its evidence.

## The findings file

`.leanprompt/findings.json` is what `npx leanprompt score --static` reads. Write it from the code as it is now, one entry per finding (the same rule in two files is two entries):

```json
{
  "v": 1,
  "findings": [
    { "rule": "LP-PC-03", "file": "src/agent/prompt.ts", "line": 12, "saves": "prefill", "severity": "high" },
    { "rule": "LP-GE-05", "file": "app/llm.py", "line": 40, "saves": "generation", "severity": "high", "fixed": true }
  ]
}
```

- `rule`: an existing rule id. An unknown id, or any malformed entry, makes `score` stop with an error that names it.
- `file`: the path relative to the project root, with forward slashes. The `ignore:` list matches on this exact string.
- `line`: the line the fix changes, a positive integer.
- `saves`: `prefill`, `generation` or `both`, from the rule's frontmatter.
- `severity`: `high`, `medium` or `low`, as the rule's "Waste signal" assigns it for the shape you found (several rules give different severities for different shapes).
- `share` (optional): the fraction, from 0 to 1, of the app's prompt tokens (prefill) or output tokens (generation) this finding wastes. Set it only when you have computed it, from the usage log or from the estimate above; otherwise leave it out and the score uses the severity's default share. `leanprompt calibrate` does not rescale a share you set.
- `fixed` (optional): `true` once the fix is applied and proved. The score skips fixed findings.

## The score

`npx leanprompt score` scores the project and writes `.leanprompt/score.json`, which is also the source of the README badge (shields.io's endpoint badge reads that file). With no flags it picks the measured score when `.leanprompt/usage.jsonl` exists and the static score from `.leanprompt/findings.json` otherwise; `--measured <file>` or `--static <file>` chooses, and `--dir <project>` points at another project. It prints `leanprompt score: <score> <kind>` with the inputs E, S and G.

- **Measured** needs at least 50 measured calls. With fewer, `score` writes nothing, prints how many measured calls it found and suggests `--static`; then run `npx leanprompt score --static`. A measured score.json records the calls it used, the unmeasured ones, and the diagnostics above.
- **Static** scores the findings file, leaving out the findings the `ignore:` list names and the ones marked `fixed`. It is an estimate from code, labelled `static` and shown with a grey badge.

Always quote the score with its kind. How the score is computed, and where the measured one stops, is in [measure-loop](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/measure-loop/SKILL.md) ("Where the measured score stops").

## Calibrating a static score

After a static score, offer a trial run with `npx leanprompt calibrate` to fit the static weights to this app. The trial run needs the usage logger in the app ([measure-loop](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/measure-loop/SKILL.md), "Log every call"; propose it as a diff and add it only on a yes) and a fixed set of realistic inputs sent through the app (several sessions with follow-up turns, at least 50 calls in total). Those are real, billed requests, so the user runs them, or you ask before running them. Then run `npx leanprompt calibrate --synthetic`, which itself makes no calls and only marks the log as a trial run; with a log of real traffic instead, run `npx leanprompt calibrate` without `--synthetic`. It compares the findings with the measured score of that log, prints the static score before and after and the two scale factors, and asks before it writes `.leanprompt/weights.json`; nothing is written without `y`, or `--yes`. Run from a tool with no terminal, it prints the comparison and writes nothing: show the user the result, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree. Static scores computed after that say `calibrated`. Once a usage log exists, plain `npx leanprompt score` gives the measured score; pass `--static` to see the calibrated static one. Method and limits: [docs/calibration.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/docs/calibration.md).

## Proving each fix

Each fix is handed over to `measure-loop`, which runs the rule's "Verify" step as a before and after on the same scenario (the provider's usage fields across two requests, and the kvsig ids that must stay equal) and rescores. The user's `fix now` covers the one diff you showed, nothing more, so ask before each of the other steps:

- **The before and after run.** Ask the user to run the scenario, or ask whether you may run it yourself, and say that it sends real requests to the provider, which are billed. Do not start it without a yes.
- **The stable-head test.** Offer it separately, as its own small diff (the new test file), and add it only after the user says yes. It keeps the fix from quietly coming undone, and it runs offline. Report the proof as one before and after line per field ([presenting.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/skills/llm-call-audit/presenting.md)), then set `fixed: true` on the finding and run `npx leanprompt score` again. A fix that can change answers (a reorder, a smaller model, less thinking, shorter outputs) needs its answer check to pass first; the rule's "Don't break meaning" says which check (E6 is why).

## Sharing a lesson

After a fix has a measured before and after, or when a rule misled the user (it did not fit their app, or the fix changed answers), offer once to share what happened as a "lesson learned". It is an offer, not a step: say what it is, and never run it unasked. Only after the user says yes, run `npx leanprompt lesson --rule <id>` (add `--before <an older score.json>` when they kept one). It writes a draft to `.leanprompt/lesson.md` on their machine from the rule id, the target and the current score; it contains no code, paths, prompts or model output, and nothing is sent anywhere.

The user reads the exact text, edits it, and opens the issue themselves: the command prints a link to the "Lesson learned" form on GitHub, which carries no text from the draft, and the user pastes the draft into the form. A maintainer reads every submission before it informs a rule, and credit is optional ([THANKYOU.md](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/blob/v0.1.0-dev/THANKYOU.md)). If `lesson` reports something to remove (a key, an email, a path, a host name), help the user remove it and run it again; do not paste the removed text anywhere. Do not offer after a static score alone: there is no before and after to share.

## What this skill does not do

It does not edit code on its own, it does not change answers to save tokens, it does not judge model choice beyond routing obviously small calls, and it does not promise a saving for an app it has not measured. Money is not in the score; when the report shows it, it uses the prices in [providers/](https://github.com/Podwarden/leanprompt/tree/v0.1.0-dev/providers/) with their checked date. Prompt Lens, an LM Warden view of where reuse broke, is planned; until it ships, the kvsig ids and the provider's usage fields are the tools.
